## 3. Gates, in order

**Gate 1 — a §2.10 signature per mode, derived before any mode is connected to any diagnosis.** Structural and instantiated differences should produce different responses to perturbation of a defended setpoint, measured as restorative effort against cost. A contributing factor has to be measurable independently of the outcome; without a signature, the only route runs backwards from pathology to inferred mode, which no individual case can check. This gate is first because everything downstream depends on it.

**Gate 2 — the ordering-inverts claim, established rather than asserted.** Divided and fragmented reaching above the unmarked case is currently a statement, not a finding. It is also the claim most likely to be quietly dropped if it proves inconvenient, which is reason to test it early.

**Gate 3 — differential contribution.** What *fragmented* contributes differently across conditions §2.8 holds to be mechanistically distinct. Without this, it is a shared category with deniability attached.

**Gate 4 — the onset/course split resolved.** *Which* pathologies arise and *how they evolve or present* have different evidence requirements. Course and presentation are third-person observable; onset attribution is not. Develop one, hold the other.

